WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(splashing)
(happy light-hearted music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.923
- [Narrator] (humming) Milk,
good old-fashioned milk

00:00:02.923 --> 00:00:05.199
in your cereal, in your
coffee, in your glass.

00:00:05.199 --> 00:00:06.418
Calcium!

00:00:06.418 --> 00:00:09.506
But the best use for
milk: dunking cookies.

00:00:09.506 --> 00:00:11.724
I mean, who doesn't love
a cookie dipped in milk?

00:00:11.724 --> 00:00:13.136
But why?

00:00:13.136 --> 00:00:16.041
Turns out it's because science says so.

00:00:16.041 --> 00:00:17.098
But before we get into it,

00:00:17.098 --> 00:00:19.030
let's take a quick look back in time.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.203
(striving dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:21.203 --> 00:00:22.587
It's 10,000 BCE.

00:00:22.587 --> 00:00:24.355
The agricultural revolution occurs,

00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:25.578
animals are domesticated,
(mooing)

00:00:25.578 --> 00:00:26.630
and animals are milked.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:27.463
(ding)
Great!

00:00:27.463 --> 00:00:28.599
(whooshing)
A lot of time goes by.

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:29.432
(whooshing)

00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:30.265
Hey, guess what?

00:00:30.265 --> 00:00:31.439
It's 1862.

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:33.525
Louis Pasteur modernizes milk safety,

00:00:33.525 --> 00:00:34.943
the term pasteurize is born.

00:00:34.943 --> 00:00:35.859
Get it?
(clinking)

00:00:35.859 --> 00:00:36.692
Maybe no?!

00:00:36.692 --> 00:00:37.525
Who cares!

00:00:37.525 --> 00:00:38.358
(whooshing)
Moving on.

00:00:38.358 --> 00:00:39.312
It's 1884!
(click)

00:00:39.312 --> 00:00:40.742
Milk is bottled up.
(splashing)

00:00:40.742 --> 00:00:42.466
The milkman became a
thing, yada, yada, yada.

00:00:42.466 --> 00:00:44.413
1994, Got Milk? ads swept the nation.

00:00:44.413 --> 00:00:46.643
Everyone's got a milk mustache, great!

00:00:46.643 --> 00:00:47.743
Now that's over.

00:00:47.743 --> 00:00:49.177
Why the H E double hockey sticks

00:00:49.177 --> 00:00:51.094
do we dunk our cookies into milk?

00:00:51.094 --> 00:00:53.047
(light bouncy orchestral music)

00:00:53.047 --> 00:00:55.012
- [Man] When you dip cookies into milk,

00:00:55.012 --> 00:00:58.046
you change a number of
things about those cookies

00:00:58.046 --> 00:01:00.465
that completely alters
your eating experience.

00:01:00.465 --> 00:01:01.298
(shuffling)

00:01:01.298 --> 00:01:03.256
- [Narrator] By alters,
Matt means, oh wait.

00:01:03.256 --> 00:01:04.645
Let me introduce you to Matt.

00:01:04.645 --> 00:01:06.622
- [Matt] Hi, my name is Matt Hartings,

00:01:06.622 --> 00:01:08.833
and I am a Professor of Chemistry.

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:11.328
And I teach a class on cooking chemistry

00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:12.973
Here at American University.

00:01:12.973 --> 00:01:13.806
(scraping)

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:14.639
- [Narrator] But back to it.

00:01:14.639 --> 00:01:17.790
By alters, Matt means when
you dip cookies into milk,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.752
not only does the texture
and flavor change,

00:01:19.752 --> 00:01:22.047
but the chemical composition does as well,

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:25.035
which is what makes scientists go wild.

00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:26.173
(moves into mysterious music)

00:01:26.173 --> 00:01:29.204
- [Matt] Chemists are
fascinated in how taste works

00:01:29.204 --> 00:01:32.932
and how all of the molecules
that you're experiencing

00:01:32.932 --> 00:01:35.347
when you're eating food,
how they all work together

00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:38.258
and lead to one thought in your brain.

00:01:38.258 --> 00:01:39.347
- [Narrator] But how?

00:01:39.347 --> 00:01:42.604
- [Matt] There are specific
ways to measure food chemistry,

00:01:42.604 --> 00:01:45.683
things like an MS-Nose that tries

00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:50.226
to break down food aromas
into their individual parts.

00:01:50.226 --> 00:01:52.023
- [Narrator] The MS-Nose is a tool

00:01:52.023 --> 00:01:53.290
that helps scientists understand

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.113
how complex molecules come
together to make flavor.

00:01:56.113 --> 00:01:58.463
(happy bouncy music)

00:01:58.463 --> 00:02:00.599
So what does all this
fancy technology tell us

00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:02.199
when you dunk that cookie?

00:02:02.199 --> 00:02:05.066
- [Matt] You add a
creaminess to your cookie,

00:02:05.066 --> 00:02:09.117
and that makes your cookie
taste completely different.

00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:10.992
We will dampen some of the flavors

00:02:10.992 --> 00:02:13.935
that are naturally sharp
in a chocolate chip cookie.

00:02:13.935 --> 00:02:15.935
It will soak up liquids.

00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:20.546
- [Narrator] So we can universally agree,

00:02:20.546 --> 00:02:21.648
dunking chocolate chip cookies

00:02:21.648 --> 00:02:23.610
into milk is the best thing ever?

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.436
- [Matt] Speaking to my own experience,

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:27.157
I am not a big dipper.

00:02:27.157 --> 00:02:27.990
(record scratching)

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.451
- [Narrator] Wait, what?

00:02:29.451 --> 00:02:31.205
- [Matt] I take a bite of my cookie

00:02:31.205 --> 00:02:33.545
and then have a drink of milk.

00:02:33.545 --> 00:02:36.817
- [Narrator] You just had to
be different, didn't you?!

00:02:36.817 --> 00:02:39.234
Still sounds delicious to me.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.063
(echoing beep)

